  "seeded": "raw seconds from one run (tests run 36054336022), not reference-normalized: the reference workload does not run in the timed job until the planner lands beside it, so this run took no reading to normalize by, and the first scheduled refresh replaces every number here with reference-normalized medians over main runs, which is why `units` is the one place that fact is recorded. A pull request's run, not main's, on purpose: main's newest fully-measured run was 97 commits stale and covered fewer suites, and the first scheduled refresh re-derives everything from main. Each suite is the mean of its measured head-round totals in that run, in raw seconds; the warm-up round is discarded by the timed job's design and the base side is another tree, so neither is counted. target_cell_weight 65: the 230 recorded weights total 804.4 s, which is 13 cells at that target; it is the smallest 5-unit step at which the planner's balance guarantee holds (heaviest cell 76.87 against a 61.23 median, margin 0.35) and the count fits the bound, so the target is measured rather than chosen. max_cells 15: the measured run ran 15 cells, the concurrency the repository runs today, so the bound is that number; when the derived count reaches it the planner clamps and names the target the margin would need. 12 of the tree's 242 suites are not measured by these runs and are estimated at the median of the recorded weights: test_gate_freshness.py, test_gate_freshness_run.py, test_speed_gate_options.py, test_tab_routing_dict_construction.py, test_tab_routing_dict_lengths.py, test_tab_routing_js_keys.py, test_tab_routing_positions.py, test_tab_routing_sequence_reads.py, test_tab_routing_set_operations.py, test_tab_routing_unhashable_read.py, test_timed_planner.py, test_timed_refresh.py Re-derive with `python3 scripts/ci/refresh_timings.py --runs-root <downloaded runs> --out .github/suite-timings.json` (--seed for the first file from one run)."
